I keep seeing suggestions about how the game could be made harder--by
removing features, adding snags, adding more ways to encounter sudden
death, imposing restrictions, etc.  Why?  At least IMHO, discovering/
learning new tricks and using them is more than half the fun of
Nethack.   The vast majority of the suggestions I see would move
Nethack a step closer to boring.

I also see some illogic in many of the suggestions.  On one hand,
people say that things like polypiling, rings of conflict, and getting
more than 3 wishes from a wand of wishing upset the game balance.
They than go on to suggest how to make the game harder in order to
restore "balance".   The same people then suggest removing those
"abuses" that upset game balance.  Think about it--does this really
make sense?  First make the game harder to counterbalance "abuses",
and then remove those "abuses".  

But even either one taken alone is a bad idea.  Making the game harder
to counterbalance polypiling would make polypiling a *requirement*.
That's ridiculous.  Polypiling is fun every once in a while, but
making hours of garbage collection a necessity for having a fighting
chance in hell is simply crazy.  On the other hand, as I just said,
"abuses" can be fun.  Removing them from the game will remove the fun. 

Here's a simple solution:  If you want a challanging game, don't
polypile, don't wear a ring of conflict in hell, and don't force more
than 3 wishes from a wand of wishing.  And if you do those things, do
them realizing that you will make the rest of the game a lot easier.
But don't remove them from the game altogether.  And don't make
winning require boring and/or esoteric procedures.  Hack was a fun
game even when the only special level was the maze and all you had to
do to get the amulet was move a rock out of a dead end.  Winning
doesn't have to be near impossible or esoteric to make the game worth
playing. 

One last thing:  ideas for future versions should be suggestions of
how to make the game more fun, not things that would be cute the first
time and boring ever after, and not ways of making the game a
perfectly balanced mathematical equation.  Maybe it's just me, but I
fail to see how introducing ways in which the most powerful of
characters can suddenly die instantly regardless of what precautions
are taken can make the game more fun.  Since such suggestions
invariably introduce blind luck, they don't make the game more
challanging--just more frustrating.

Okay, folks--let the praise, comments, ideas, and flames roll in!  ;)

